ail>dipinte.gupta@wsu.edu</email> (D.G.); <email>oinamw@gmail.com</email> (O.W.S.); <email>suryamanik94@gmail.com</email> (S.M.)</aff><aff id="af2-plants-15-03237"><label>2</label>Department of Plant Pathology, <institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/05dk0ce17</institution-id><institution>Washington State University</institution></institution-wrap>, Pullman, WA 99164, USA; <email>prabu.gnanasekaran@wsu.edu</email></aff><aff id="af3-plants-15-03237"><label>3</label>Indian Council of Agriculture Research, <institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/03cbgzw80</institution-id><institution>Central Institute for Cotton Research</institution></institution-wrap>, Regional Station, Sirsa 125055, Haryana, India</aff><aff id="af4-plants-15-03237"><label>4</label>Indian Council of Agriculture Research, Indian Institute of Agricultural Biotechnology, Ranchi 834003, Jharkhand, India</aff><author-notes><corresp id="c1-plants-15-03237"><label>*</label>Correspondence: <email>anirban@iari.res.in</email> (A.R.); <email>bikash.mandal@icar.gov.in</email> (B.M.)</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>21</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>10</month><year>2026</year></pub-date><volume>15</volume><issue>20</issue><issue-id pub-id-type="pmc-issue-id">524151</issue-id><elocation-id>3237</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>04</day><month>9</month><year>2026</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>08</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>15</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></date></history><pub-history><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>21</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>27</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2026-10-27 00:17:13.423"><day>27</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></date></event></pub-history><permissions><copyright-statement>© 2026 by the authors.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2026</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbylicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license</ext-link>.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="plants-15-03237.pdf"><?pdf-name plants-15-03237.pdf?><?pdf-size 6649507?><?pdf-md5 33806536d598424bbe0da0f46e217c5b?><?pdf-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?pdf-cloudpmc-urn urn:app:fe2f/13709202/33806536d598/plants-15-03237.pdf?></self-uri><abstract><p>Watermelon production is significantly affected by watermelon bud necrosis virus (WBNV), an economically important orthotospovirus in the Indian subcontinent. RNA interference (RNAi)-based approaches offer promising strategies for developing broad-spectrum antiviral resistance in susceptible plants. In this study, we evaluated the efficacy of foliar application of double-stranded RNA (dsRNA) and hairpin RNA (hpRNA) derived from the full-length <italic toggle="yes">NSs</italic> gene of WBNV against WBNV infection in two host plants, <italic toggle="yes">Nicotiana benthamiana</italic> and cowpea (<italic toggle="yes">Vigna unguiculata</italic>). Fluorescein-labeled dsRNA of <italic toggle="yes">NSs</italic> gene (dsNSs) was tracked in <italic toggle="yes">N. benthamiana</italic> plants, where dsNSs translocated from the treated leaf to adjacent leaves, stem, and roots. Naked dsNSs was no longer detectable in treated leaves at 5 days post-inoculation (dpi), whereas mesoporous silica nanoparticle-conjugated dsNSs (dsNSs-MSNP) remained detectable in the treated leaves beyond 5 dpi, indicating enhanced stability. Following WBNV inoculation, <italic toggle="yes">N. benthamiana</italic> plants treated with hairpin of <italic toggle="yes">NSs</italic> gene (hpNSs), dsNSs, and dsNSs-MSNP exhibited 112, 303, and 370 fold reduction in viral RNA accumulation, respectively. Although these treatments delayed symptom development, all inoculated plants eventually showed the viral symptoms in <italic toggle="yes">N. benthamiana</italic>. Similarly, treatment of <italic toggle="yes">Vigna unguiculata</italic> cv. Pusa Komal (cowpea) plants with hpNSs, dsNSs, and dsNSs-MSNP resulted in reduced viral load, where dsNSs-MSNP provided the maximum reduction up to 208 fold. The dsNSs provided 100% reduction in development of systemic symptoms of bud necrosis disease in the treated cowpea plants. Collectively, these findings demonstrate that foliar application of nanoparticle-conjugated dsRNA targeting the full-length <italic toggle="yes">NSs</italic> gene can effectively suppress WBNV infection and provide enhanced protection against disease development in cowpea.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>WBNV</kwd><kwd>RNAi</kwd><kwd>dsRNA</kwd><kwd>mesoporous silica nanoparticle</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group><funding-source>Science and Engineering Research Board (SERB), Department of Science and Technology (DST), Government of India (GOI)</funding-source><award-id>PDF/2021/003984</award-id></award-group><funding-statement>The study was supported under NPDF grant, sanction order number PDF/2021/003984 from the Science and Engineering Research Board (SERB), Department of Science and Technology (DST), Government of India (GOI).</funding-statement></funding-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatus</meta-name><meta-value>0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-st